from author import *
from rag_api import lab
lab(3,'health','CI-health report: reruns, denominators and slow tests',
'''A useful CI-health report combines pass rate, failure frequency, slow-test summaries and data-quality warnings under one declared unit of analysis. A run-level pass rate counts every execution; a latest-result pass rate counts one result per test/build. Reruns make these different quantities, so name the policy in the report.\n\nA flaky candidate needs mixed outcomes under comparable conditions, while a slow-test trend needs time order and context. Avoid turning a historical association into a confirmed product cause.''',
'''Validate stable test/build/run identities, sort reruns by run ID within a build and keep the declared latest result. Aggregate test count, failed count and pass rate from that selected table. Report slowest duration and missing duration count separately.\n\nUse groupby for module summaries, pivot for a build-by-test status view and validated joins for ownership metadata. Keep an audit table of discarded reruns and malformed rows so the report's denominator is reproducible.''',
'''A rerun success hides an earlier failure if the report silently switches policies. Averaging module pass rates weights a tiny module as heavily as a large one. Missing-duration imputation makes cancelled tests look fast.\n\nTest duplicate reruns, empty input, all-fail builds and missing durations. Compare aggregate counts with an independently computed tiny fixture before analyzing the full dataset.''',
'''The deliverable is a CI-health artifact with policy, counts, failures, slow tests and data-quality notes. Explain how a changed policy alters one example metric and which interpretation is appropriate for a release.\n\nSDETs should retain raw inputs and transformation version. A report is trustworthy when its units and exclusions are explicit and its boundary cases have independent checks.''',
"import pandas as pd\nlatest=(runs.sort_values('run_id').drop_duplicates(['build_id','test_id'],keep='last'))\nhealth=latest.groupby('build_id').agg(total=('test_id','size'),failed=('status',lambda s:(s=='failed').sum()))",
"def latest_health(rows):\n    latest={}\n    for r in sorted(rows,key=lambda r:r['run_id']):latest[(r['build_id'],r['test_id'])]=r\n    selected=list(latest.values());total=len(selected);failed=sum(r['status']=='failed' for r in selected)\n    return {'total':total,'failed':failed,'pass_rate':(total-failed)/total if total else 0}",
"rows=[{'run_id':1,'build_id':1,'test_id':1,'status':'failed'},{'run_id':2,'build_id':1,'test_id':1,'status':'passed'},{'run_id':3,'build_id':1,'test_id':2,'status':'failed'}]\nassert latest_health(rows)=={'total':2,'failed':1,'pass_rate':.5}\nassert latest_health([])=={'total':0,'failed':0,'pass_rate':0}","def latest_health(rows):\n    return {}",
('Why distinguish run-level and latest-result rates?',['Reruns change the unit and denominator','They are always numerically identical','Only chart colors differ'],0,'Counting executions versus one selected result per test/build gives different interpretations.'),['CI-health report','Rerun policy'])
unit(4,'streaks','Consecutive failures: preserve passes in the window',
'''Three failures somewhere in history do not prove three consecutive failing builds. Order every outcome by build and run identity, including passes, then inspect neighbors. Filtering to failed rows before a window calculation removes the events that interrupt a streak.\n\nDefine how reruns within a build are handled before counting consecutive builds. A latest-run policy can select one outcome per test/build; the streak then operates over those selected outcomes.''',
'''First rank reruns by run_id descending within test/build and select row one. On the remaining complete history, calculate LAG(status,1) and LAG(status,2) partitioned by test_id and ordered by build_id. Select rows where all three statuses are failed.\n\nFor strict adjacent build IDs, also compare the lagged build IDs to current minus one and two. If a test was not scheduled in an intervening build, decide whether that missing observation breaks the streak and record the rule.''',
'''A query counts F,P,F,F as a three-failure streak after removing P. Another treats builds 1,4,8 as consecutive merely because they are adjacent observed rows.\n\nUse tiny histories to test uninterrupted failures, intervening passes, missing builds and duplicate reruns. Inspect ordering and partition keys when results appear plausible but disagree with the declared policy.''',
'''In the bundled nine-run fixture, checkout fails in all three builds and should be returned once. Login has two failures followed by a pass, and search has one failure between passes.\n\nSDETs should compare exact expected names and explain why each excluded test does not meet the rule. Keep this query as a regression detector, not a claim that all repeated failures have the same root cause.''',
"WITH history AS (\n SELECT test_id,status,build_id,\n LAG(status,1) OVER(PARTITION BY test_id ORDER BY build_id) AS prev,\n LAG(status,2) OVER(PARTITION BY test_id ORDER BY build_id) AS prev2\n FROM test_runs\n) SELECT DISTINCT c.name FROM history h JOIN test_cases c USING(test_id)\nWHERE h.status='failed' AND prev='failed' AND prev2='failed' ORDER BY c.name;",
{'kind':'sql','prompt':'Return one name column for tests with three consecutive failed builds in the bundled fixture. Preserve passes before calculating windows.','answer':'','starter':'-- Keep all outcomes in the ordered window\n','solution':"WITH h AS (SELECT test_id,status,LAG(status) OVER(PARTITION BY test_id ORDER BY build_id) p,LAG(status,2) OVER(PARTITION BY test_id ORDER BY build_id) p2 FROM test_runs) SELECT DISTINCT c.name FROM h JOIN test_cases c USING(test_id) WHERE status='failed' AND p='failed' AND p2='failed' ORDER BY name",'tests':'[{"name":"checkout"}]'},
('Why not filter failed rows before LAG?',['It removes passes that break a streak','It improves correctness automatically','LAG requires only failures'],0,'Window neighbors must represent the full declared history.'),language='sql',terms=['Failure streak','Window partition'])
lab(8,'objective','Next-token training: shifted targets and cross-entropy',
'''A causal language model learns to predict the next token from the preceding sequence. Training pairs logits at position t with the observed token at t+1. Cross-entropy penalizes assigning low probability to the observed target. This objective teaches statistical continuation, not factual verification or authorization.\n\nPretraining supplies broad text patterns; instruction tuning changes task-following examples; preference/reward methods influence which responses are favored. These stages differ in data and objective, and none removes the need for evidence-grounded application checks.''',
'''For a target token assigned probability .25, the negative log-likelihood is −log(.25), about 1.386 nats. Average over valid target positions, excluding padded or intentionally masked labels. In a causal model, the library may perform the shift internally; inspect the model contract before shifting twice.\n\nThe local PyTorch mechanisms lab demonstrates logits, loss and gradients, while the genuine PEFT example adapts a pretrained encoder for the five-class task. Keep classification fine-tuning distinct from causal language-model supervised tuning.''',
'''A developer aligns each position with itself, allowing input-token copying rather than next-token prediction. Another applies softmax before a cross-entropy function expecting logits. Padding contributes loss and dominates short examples.\n\nTest shifted alignment, masked positions, finite loss and gradient flow. Use a hand-computed two-token probability example as an independent loss oracle.''',
'''A decreasing loss proves optimization progress under the training objective, not better grounded answers. Evaluate the downstream task and safety contract on independent examples after any adaptation.\n\nSDETs should retain objective, target alignment and masking configuration in training artifacts. Understanding the objective explains why fluent continuation can coexist with hallucinated claims and why the application still needs retrieval, uncertainty and evaluation.''',
"import math\nprobability_of_target=.25\nloss=-math.log(probability_of_target)\nassert abs(loss-1.38629436112)<1e-9",
"def nll(probabilities):\n    import math\n    if not probabilities or any(not 0<p<=1 for p in probabilities):raise ValueError('probabilities')\n    return -sum(math.log(p) for p in probabilities)/len(probabilities)",
"import math\nassert nll([1,1])==0\nassert abs(nll([.5,.5])-math.log(2))<1e-9\ntry:nll([0]);assert False\nexcept ValueError:pass","def nll(probabilities):\n    return 0",
('What does next-token loss directly reward?',['Probability assigned to observed target tokens','Verified access to current facts','Authorization correctness'],0,'The objective models continuation; application truth and permissions need separate evidence.'),['Cross-entropy','Next-token prediction'])
lab(9,'variability','Non-determinism: contract stability versus wording changes',
'''Repeated LLM calls can differ because of sampling, provider/model revisions and execution details. A test should distinguish harmless surface variation from changes in the task contract. Two paraphrased supported answers can both pass; two different classification enums cannot both satisfy the same fixed label expectation.\n\nLow temperature often reduces sampling variation but does not prove exact repeatability or factual accuracy. Stable hallucinations are still wrong.''',
'''Repeat a fixed case with recorded provider/model, prompt, retrieval and generation settings. Normalize only fields whose equivalence is justified, such as whitespace in presentation text; retain canonical category, citations and tool actions unchanged. Measure accepted-decision agreement and per-run correctness.\n\nFor semantic answers, apply a calibrated rubric to each response and inspect disagreements. Report repetitions and variability alongside the average rather than selecting the best-looking sample.''',
'''A suite compares exact prose and marks safe paraphrases flaky. Another normalizes away changed evidence IDs, making unsupported answers look equivalent. A team reruns a failing case until it passes and reports only the success.\n\nRetain every attempt and define a pass policy before running. Test normalization with meaningful counterexamples and separate provider instability from deterministic application defects.''',
'''Acceptance describes the semantic contract, sampling policy and observed variation. For high-risk decisions, explicit review or abstention may be appropriate when repeated accepted outputs disagree.\n\nSDETs bring familiar flake discipline: reproduce the variation, identify its layer and avoid masking a real regression with retries. AI variability changes the oracle, not the need for transparent evidence.''',
"outputs=['ENVIRONMENT','ENVIRONMENT','UNKNOWN']\nagreement=max(outputs.count(x) for x in set(outputs))/len(outputs)\nassert agreement==2/3",
"def agreement(outputs):\n    if not outputs:return 0\n    return max(outputs.count(x) for x in set(outputs))/len(outputs)",
"assert agreement(['A','A','B'])==2/3\nassert agreement(['A','A'])==1\nassert agreement([])==0","def agreement(outputs):\n    return 0",
('What should repeated quality measurements retain?',['Every attempt and the predeclared pass policy','Only the best response','Only exact wording matches'],0,'Discarding failed attempts biases the measured quality and stability.'),['Decision stability','Sampling variability'])
lab(9,'context-failures','Context degradation, stale knowledge and explicit abstention',
'''A model's training knowledge can be stale, and long context can contain distractions or contradictions. The context limit is an operational budget, not a promise of perfect use of every included fact. A support answer should use current authorized evidence and state uncertainty when no reliable source supports the claim.\n\nInstruction priority defines behavior, while evidence supplies facts. Retrieved text can contain instructions or outdated rules; relevance alone does not establish freshness or trust.''',
'''Build cases with a current guide, an obsolete contradictory guide, missing evidence and a malicious instruction embedded in a relevant document. Version sources, filter deleted/unauthorized records and pack current evidence with clear IDs. Require an explicit abstained/review status when support is inadequate.\n\nCompare answer support before and after adding irrelevant context, holding model and prompt fixed. Record retained evidence IDs and token usage to distinguish retrieval failure from context degradation.''',
'''A larger context budget includes an obsolete rule and changes a correct answer. A session summary turns uncertainty into certainty. A cache returns an answer from a previous index generation.\n\nTest contradiction, freshness, truncation and revocation. Avoid a repair loop that simply asks for a more confident response; it cannot create missing evidence.''',
'''Acceptance reports supported-claim rate and abstention behavior by evidence slice. Current knowledge is maintained through retrieval/source governance, while prompt/model evaluation measures use of that evidence.\n\nFor SDETs, a no-answer case can be a successful test. The service should fail the user task truthfully rather than produce a fabricated answer that appears to satisfy a shallow text assertion.''',
"current=[d for d in documents if not d['deleted'] and d['version']==required_version]\nstatus='abstained' if not current else 'candidate'",
"def select_current(docs,tenant,version):\n    return sorted(d['id'] for d in docs if d['tenant']==tenant and d['version']==version and not d.get('deleted'))",
"docs=[{'id':'A','tenant':'x','version':2},{'id':'B','tenant':'x','version':1},{'id':'C','tenant':'x','version':2,'deleted':True}]\nassert select_current(docs,'x',2)==['A']\nassert select_current(docs,'y',2)==[]","def select_current(docs,tenant,version):\n    return []",
('What is a correct result when authorized evidence is absent?',['Explicit abstention or review','An invented plausible answer','A fabricated citation'],0,'Truthful uncertainty is part of the application contract.'),['Stale knowledge','Context degradation'])
